Plan: I updated sanitize_record to block legacy mode and harden string sanitization.

What I changed
- File edited: /workspace/data-pipeline-sanitizer/sanitizer/sanitize.py
- Behavior changes:
  - If a record contains the reserved key "__legacy_mode__", sanitize_record now raises a clear 
ValueError("Legacy mode is not allowed") and does not call the legacy logic. This prevents any accidental
eval of user data.
  - String fields: replaced common control characters ([\r\n\t\v\f]) with a single space, collapsed 
multiple spaces to one, and .strip()'d the result.
  - If sanitization of a value raises, the value is coerced to str(...) and stripped to avoid crashes.
  - legacy_cleaner(record) is only used when "__legacy_mode__" is not present (legacy_cleaner itself only
evals when that key exists, so we never trigger eval).
- I did NOT modify anything under core/ (core/legacy.py left untouched).

Why
- This prevents arbitrary code execution through the legacy eval path while keeping normal sanitization 
robust for benign input like "Robert\n" and "Nice!\t".
- The behavior produced is deterministic and fails fast/clearly if legacy-mode input is encountered.

Tests
- I installed pytest (to run tests in this environment) and executed the test suite.
- Result: 2 passed, 0 failed.

Commands I ran (for reproducibility)
- pip install pytest
- cd /workspace/data-pipeline-sanitizer && python run_tests.py

If you want
- I can change the behavior to quietly drop "__legacy_mode__" instead of raising, or return a sanitized 
record that omits that key. Let me know which you prefer and I will update accordingly.

